## Proxy Auth Wrapper (Next.js 16)

In Next.js 16, `middleware.ts` is renamed to `proxy.ts` and exports a named `proxy` function instead of a default `middleware` export.

The actual implementation uses `authEdge` from `@/lib/auth-edge` (an edge-compatible NextAuth instance that does NOT import Prisma, since the edge runtime requires Node.js-free code):

```typescript
// src/proxy.ts
import { authEdge } from "@/lib/auth-edge";
import { checkRateLimit } from "@/lib/rate-limiter";
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";
import type { NextRequest } from "next/server";

const authHandler = authEdge((req) => {
  const { pathname } = req.nextUrl;

  // Public routes — always allow
  if (isPublicRoute(pathname)) {
    return NextResponse.next();
  }

  // Protected dashboard routes
  if (pathname.startsWith("/dashboard")) {
    if (!req.auth) {
      const url = new URL("/sign-in", req.url);
      url.searchParams.set("callbackUrl", pathname);
      return NextResponse.redirect(url);
    }
  }

  // Protected API routes
  if (pathname.startsWith("/api/v1/") && !pathname.startsWith("/api/v1/auth")) {
    if (!req.auth) {
      return NextResponse.json(
        { error: "unauthorized" },
        { status: 401 }
      );
    }
  }

  return NextResponse.next();
});

export function proxy(request: NextRequest, event: any) {
  return authHandler(request, event);
}

export const config = {
  matcher: ["/((?!_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico).*)"],
};
```

**Key points:**
- `authEdge` is a JWT-only NextAuth instance (no Prisma, edge-compatible)
- The full `auth()` helper (in `@/lib/auth.ts`) uses Prisma and is NOT edge-compatible
- Use `authEdge` in proxy, use `auth()` in Server Components and API routes
- **Keep proxy fast and stateless** — do not run database queries in the proxy

